5. Explain the concept of a classification report in machine learning. Why is it used and what type of models require it?

A classification report in machine learning is a detailed summary of how well a classification model performs, 
showing precision, recall, F1‑score, and support for each class. It is used to evaluate models beyond simple accuracy, 
especially when dealing with imbalanced datasets or multi‑class problems.

Classification models such as:
Logistic Regression
Decision Trees & Random Forests
Support Vector Machines (SVM)
k‑Nearest Neighbors (kNN)
Neural Networks for classification
Not used for regression models (they use metrics like RMSE, MAE, R²).

A classification report is essential whenever you train a classification model. It gives a complete picture of performance per class,
helping you avoid the trap of relying only on accuracy.
