6. In a classification report, explain the meaning of the following metrics:
Precision -
Definition: Of all the samples predicted as a certain class, how many were actually correct.
Formula: Precision=𝑇𝑃/𝑇𝑃+𝐹𝑃
Use case: Important when false positives are costly (e.g., predicting spam emails incorrectly).

Recall -
Definition: Of all the samples that truly belong to a class, how many were correctly identified.
Formula: Recall=𝑇𝑃/𝑇𝑃+𝐹𝑁
Use case: Important when false negatives are costly (e.g., missing a cancer diagnosis).

F1 Score -
Definition: The harmonic mean of precision and recall, balancing both.
Formula:𝐹1=2⋅Precision*Recall/Precision+Recall
Use case: Useful when you need a single metric that considers both false positives and false negatives.

Support -
Definition: The number of actual samples for each class in the dataset.
Example: If you have 50 samples of class A, its support = 50.
Use case: Helps you understand class distribution and whether metrics are reliable (low support → less reliable).

Accuracy - 
Definition: The overall proportion of correctly predicted samples.
Formula: Accuracy=𝑇𝑃+𝑇𝑁/𝑇𝑃+𝑇𝑁+𝐹𝑃+𝐹𝑁
Use case: Works well when classes are balanced, but can be misleading with imbalanced datasets.
